From 9417c5818a0146980c2608fda94c908e604eb033 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Laxman Acharya Padhya <acharyalaxman8848@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 21 Jul 2026 10:17:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: mt76: mt7921: validate CLC firmware records
 (p03: degrade instead of failing init)

Backport of upstream commit 9417c5818a0146980c2608fda94c908e604eb033
("wifi: mt76: mt7921: validate CLC firmware records"), not present in
Fedora 7.2.3 / openSUSE 7.2, with one deliberate change described below.

mt7921_load_clc() walks the CLC (country/location config) records
appended to the MCU firmware image and caches each in phy->clc[], which
is declared in mt792x.h as

  void *clc[MT792x_CLC_MAX_NUM];   /* MT792x_CLC_MAX_NUM == 3 */

The index used is clc->idx, taken verbatim from the firmware blob and
never validated before subscripting that array, so a record with
idx >= 3 writes a heap pointer past the end of the array and corrupts
the adjacent members of struct mt792x_phy. Upstream also validates the
region table against the firmware size, uses underflow-safe length
comparisons, checks that a full record header is present before
dereferencing it, and caches clc->len for the loop increment, which
additionally fixes an infinite loop when a record reports len == 0.

p03 change: upstream makes every one of those record checks abort
mt7921_load_clc() with -EINVAL. That return value propagates through
mt7921_run_firmware() and mt7921e_mcu_init() to mt7921_init_work(),
which gives up with

  mt7921e 0000:05:00.0: hardware init failed

and the interface never appears.

On a Framework Laptop 16 (MT7922, ASIC revision 79220010, WM firmware
build 20260724143402) the shipping firmware really does carry a record
with idx == 3, which is what the pre-patch kernels report:

  UBSAN: array-index-out-of-bounds in .../mt7921/mcu.c:471:7
  index 3 is out of range for type 'void *[3]'
  Workqueue: events mt7921_init_work [mt7921_common]
   __ubsan_handle_out_of_bounds+0xab/0xb0
   mt7921_run_firmware+0x445/0x5a0 [mt7921_common]

so on that machine the upstream patch converts a memory-corruption bug
into a total loss of wifi. Confirmed by A/B on the one laptop:
7.2.2.p03.25 and 7.2.3.p03.28 log the UBSAN splat above and still bring
up wlan0, while 7.2.3.p03.29 - identical but for this patch - prints
"hardware init failed" and never creates an interface.

Failing the whole device is also inconsistent with the rest of the
function. mt7921_load_clc() already returns 0 when the firmware
contains no CLC region at all ("if (!clc_base) goto out"),
__mt7921_mcu_set_clc() starts with "if (!clc) return 0", and
mt7921_regd_init() does not look at phy->clc[] - the driver is built to
run with some or all of these records missing. CLC is optional
regulatory metadata, so a record it cannot parse should cost the
records themselves, not the adapter.

So the bounds are kept and the reaction is softened:

  - trailing bytes too short for a record header end the loop; that is
    padding at the end of the region, not corruption
  - a record whose length underflows sizeof(*clc) or overruns the
    region ends the loop, keeping the records parsed before it (this
    still forecloses the len == 0 infinite loop)
  - a record whose idx is beyond phy->clc[] is skipped, and parsing
    continues - that is a CLC type a newer firmware knows about and
    this driver does not, which is what the Framework 16 image
    contains

Every memory-safety property of the upstream patch is retained: no
out-of-bounds read of a record header, no out-of-bounds write to
phy->clc[], no over-long devm_kmemdup(), and no infinite loop. The
region-table checks keep upstream's fatal -EINVAL, since a bad region
table means the image itself is unusable and both paths log a message.

Fixes: 23bdc5d8cadf ("wifi: mt76: mt7921: introduce Country Location Control support")
Signed-off-by: Laxman Acharya Padhya <acharyalaxman8848@gmail.com>
Signed-off-by: Felix Fietkau <nbd@nbd.name>
---
 drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76/mt7921/mcu.c | 36 ++++++++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 32 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76/mt7921/mcu.c b/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76/mt7921/mcu.c
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76/mt7921/mcu.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76/mt7921/mcu.c
@@ -415,7 +415,8 @@
 	struct mt76_dev *mdev = &dev->mt76;
 	struct mt792x_phy *phy = &dev->phy;
 	const struct firmware *fw;
-	int ret, i, len, offset = 0;
+	size_t clc_len, fw_data_len, len, offset = 0;
+	int ret, i;
 	u8 *clc_base = NULL, hw_encap = 0;
 
 	dev->phy.clc_chan_conf = 0xff;
@@ -441,13 +442,21 @@
 	}
 
 	hdr = (const void *)(fw->data + fw->size - sizeof(*hdr));
+	if (hdr->n_region > (fw->size - sizeof(*hdr)) / sizeof(*region)) {
+		dev_err(mdev->dev, "Invalid firmware region table\n");
+		ret = -EINVAL;
+		goto out;
+	}
+	fw_data_len = fw->size - sizeof(*hdr) -
+		      hdr->n_region * sizeof(*region);
+
 	for (i = 0; i < hdr->n_region; i++) {
 		region = (const void *)((const u8 *)hdr -
 					(hdr->n_region - i) * sizeof(*region));
 		len = le32_to_cpu(region->len);
 
 		/* check if we have valid buffer size */
-		if (offset + len > fw->size) {
+		if (len > fw_data_len - offset) {
 			dev_err(mdev->dev, "Invalid firmware region\n");
 			ret = -EINVAL;
 			goto out;
@@ -464,8 +473,27 @@
 	if (!clc_base)
 		goto out;
 
-	for (offset = 0; offset < len; offset += le32_to_cpu(clc->len)) {
+	for (offset = 0; offset < len; offset += clc_len) {
+		/* trailing bytes too short for a record header: padding at the
+		 * end of the region, not an error
+		 */
+		if (len - offset < sizeof(*clc))
+			break;
+
 		clc = (const struct mt7921_clc *)(clc_base + offset);
+		clc_len = le32_to_cpu(clc->len);
+
+		/* stop at a record that overruns the region or cannot advance
+		 * the loop, keeping whatever was parsed before it
+		 */
+		if (clc_len < sizeof(*clc) || clc_len > len - offset)
+			break;
+
+		/* a CLC type this driver does not know about: skip it, but
+		 * keep parsing the records that follow
+		 */
+		if (clc->idx >= ARRAY_SIZE(phy->clc))
+			continue;
 
 		/* do not init buf again if chip reset triggered */
 		if (phy->clc[clc->idx])
@@ -477,7 +505,7 @@
 			continue;
 
 		phy->clc[clc->idx] = devm_kmemdup(mdev->dev, clc,
-						  le32_to_cpu(clc->len),
+						  clc_len,
 						  GFP_KERNEL);
 
 		if (!phy->clc[clc->idx]) {
